<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prithvi Narayan Shah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1723">1723</a>–<a href="/years/1775">1775</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prithvi Narayan Shah was king of Gorkha from 1743 and the founder of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>. He took Makwanpur in 1762 and blockaded and conquered the three kingdoms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a>, Kantipur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalitpur">Lalitpur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhaktapur">Bhaktapur</a>, in 1768–1769, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a> his capital. His sayings, the Dibya Upadesh, set out a policy of caution towards the British and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prithvi-narayan-shah.jpg" alt="Prithvi Narayan Shah" width="320" height="475" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: the Record · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Prithvi_Narayan_Shah.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Prithvi Narayan Shah</b> called his kingdom ‘a yam between two boulders’, squeezed between China and British India, a phrase still quoted in debates on Nepal’s foreign policy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Prithvi Narayan Shah</b>, who took the throne in 1743, blockaded the Kathmandu Valley's trade, took the Kingdom of Makwanpur in 1762 and Kirtipur in 1767, and armed his soldiers with muskets.</blockquote><a href="/state/gorkha-kingdom">Gorkha Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Nepal's <b>Prithvi Narayan Shah</b> conquered Bhaktapur in 1769, after taking Kathmandu and Patan; Ranajit Malla, its last king, went into exile at Varanasi.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-bhaktapur">Kingdom of Bhaktapur</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Prithvi Narayan Shah</b> of the Gorkha Kingdom captured Kathmandu during the Indra Jatra festival in 1768, while Jaya Prakash Malla fled.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kantipur">Kingdom of Kantipur</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Prithvi Narayan Shah</b> of the Gorkha Kingdom captured Patan in 1768, shortly after Kathmandu, and its last ruler Tej Narsingh Malla fled to Bhaktapur.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lalitpur">Kingdom of Lalitpur</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Prithvi Narayan Shah's</b> conquests, Lamjung was annexed by the Kingdom of Nepal in 1782.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lamjung">Kingdom of Lamjung</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Gorkha District, in what is now <a href="/countries/nepal">Nepal</a>. Died in Devighat, in what is now <a href="/countries/nepal">Nepal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=28.28,84.68&amp;year=1756" aria-label="Gorkha District on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6767 1128 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Prithvi Narayan Shah"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7217 1398h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7217 1398h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=28.28,84.68&amp;year=1756">What was here in 1756? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
